gomad: count a shared target once and prune unshared pool entries
- artifact.RetainedBytes states the stored-bytes rule: per-artifact stored
  bytes keep the target, and a sum over artifacts kept together counts a
  shared target once
- the corpus byte cap, the shard-merge evidence limits, and the minimized
  artifact default follow the rule; no limit changes
- PruneTargetPool removes pool entries no artifact links to; qualification
  pruning and the corpus call it after they remove artifacts
- a publisher creates its pool entry again when a pruner took it before
  the link
- gomad inspect reports whether an artifact's target is shared

